Problems found
==============

1. CORS is open to the entire Internet  
   app.use(cors({ origin: '*' }));

   • Security impact  
     – Any web site can make authenticated (if you ever turn on credentials) or unauthenticated calls to your API, which makes XSS or CSRF‐style data theft far easier.  
     – You lose the ability to restrict traffic to the front-end(s) you actually control.  

   • Functional impact  
     – If later in the project you decide to send cookies (credentials: true) the browser will silently refuse because Access-Control-Allow-Origin:* is incompatible with Access-Control-Allow-Credentials:true.  
     – Debugging “works in Postman, fails in browser” cases becomes harder.

2. Body-parser limit is excessive and global  
   app.use(express.json({ limit: '50mb' }));

   • Security impact  
     – A 50 MiB request is read into RAM before you even reach your own code → easy DoS vector (CPU + RAM exhaustion).  
     – The JSON parser runs on the event loop thread; a couple of huge/broken JSON payloads can block the entire server.  

   • Functional impact  
     – Almost no REST/GraphQL endpoint legitimately needs 50 MiB of JSON. When large payloads are required they are usually files, which should be streamed or handled with a multipart/form-data library (e.g. multer, busboy).

3. Middleware is mounted for every single request  
   – CORS and JSON parsing happen even for static files, health checks, etc. This is unnecessary work and increases latency.

4. Missing complementary security middleware  
   – No helmet(), rate-limiting, or input sanitisation is shown. While not strictly a “bug”, their absence makes the attack surface larger than necessary.

How to fix / Improve
====================

1. Restrict CORS to the domains that actually need it

```js
const allowedOrigins = [
  'https://app.example.com',
  'https://admin.example.com'
];

app.use(
  cors({
    origin: function (origin, cb) {
      // Allow requests with no origin (e.g. mobile apps, curl)
      if (!origin) return cb(null, true);
      if (allowedOrigins.includes(origin)) return cb(null, true);
      return cb(new Error('Not allowed by CORS'), false);
    },
    methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE', 'OPTIONS'],
    allowedHeaders: ['Content-Type', 'Authorization'],
    credentials: true,           // only if you really need cookies/headers
    optionsSuccessStatus: 204
  })
);
```

Store the whitelist in an environment variable so you don’t re-deploy to add a new domain.

2. Reduce and scope the JSON body limit

```js
// Only apply JSON parsing on routes that really need it
app.post('/api/submit',
  express.json({ limit: '1mb', strict: true }),   // 1 MiB is already large
  submitController
);

// If you genuinely expect bigger uploads, switch to streaming:
const multer = require('multer');
app.post('/api/upload', multer({ limits: { fileSize: 10 * 1024 * 1024 } }).single('file'), uploadController);
```

3. Place low-cost middleware first, expensive middleware only where needed

```js
// cheap, global
app.use(helmet());
app.use(rateLimiter);

// expensive / route-specific
app.post('/api/*', express.json({ limit: '1mb' }), router);
```

4. Add defensive layers

```js
const helmet = require('helmet');
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

app.use(helmet());

app.use(rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,   // 15 minutes
  max: 1000                   // adjust to your traffic profile
}));
```

5. Monitor and log CORS rejections & body-parser errors

```js
app.use(function (err, req, res, next) {
  if (err instanceof SyntaxError && err.status === 400 && 'body' in err) {
    console.error('Invalid JSON:', err);
    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid JSON' });
  }
  if (err.message === 'Not allowed by CORS') {
    console.warn(`Blocked CORS request from ${req.headers.origin}`);
    return res.status(403).send('Forbidden');
  }
  next(err);
});
```

Take-away checklist
-------------------

• Never ship CORS '*' to production—whitelist or dynamically validate origins.  
• Keep JSON body limits small; stream or use multipart for big uploads.  
• Mount heavy middleware only where required.  
• Add helmet(), rate-limiting, and centralised error handling.
